This week on Hidden Brain, how a series of anomalies in the way people get money, spend money and save money led to the development of the field of behavioral economics. Every day of our lives we make decisions both trivial and momentous. What will we eat for lunch today? Will we ride the elevator or take the stairs? How much money will we save from our paycheck and how much will we spend? It turns out there's a hidden logic to the way we make such choices, a logic often not rooted in rationality. Richard Thaler is a behavioural economist at the University of Chicago. He has spent decades studying the psychology of our everyday choices.
